22:52:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Your respiratory system doesn’t directly “sniff” the oxygen in the air. Instead, it continuously monitors the **levels of oxygen, carbon dioxide, and acidity in your blood** using specialized sensors called **chemoreceptors**. Those signals are sent to the brainstem, which adjusts your breathing rate and depth to keep everything in balance. Here’s how that works in practice: ### 1. The brain’s carbon dioxide (CO₂) sensors are the main controller Under normal conditions, your brain cares less about oxygen and far more about **CO₂**. When you hold your breath, CO₂ builds up in your blood. This makes your cerebrospinal fluid slightly more acidic. - **Central chemoreceptors** in your medulla oblongata (part of the brainstem) detect that pH change within seconds. - They send an urgent signal to the respiratory muscles: “Breathe faster and deeper!” This CO₂/pH loop is what makes you gasp when you’ve been underwater too long—it’s a warning that CO₂ is rising, not that oxygen is dangerously low. ### 2. Backup oxygen sensors stand guard Although CO₂ is the primary drive, your body also has **peripheral chemoreceptors** that specifically check oxygen levels. They’re located in the **carotid bodies** (in your neck) and **aortic bodies** (near your heart). These sensors are sensitive to the partial pressure of oxygen (PaO₂) in arterial blood. When the oxygen level in your blood falls significantly—most noticeably when PaO₂ drops below about **60 mmHg** (normal is ~75–100 mmHg)—the peripheral chemoreceptors fire a “low oxygen” alarm. That signal travels to the brainstem and stimulates breathing, even if CO₂ is normal. This system is exactly what kicks in at high altitude, where the air has less oxygen. You start breathing more heavily not because of CO₂, but because the carotid bodies detect low blood oxygen and increase ventilation. ### 3. The lungs and air passages also provide feedback Your airways and lungs have **stretch receptors** that tell the brain when they are fully inflated, preventing over-inflation (the Hering–Breuer reflex). Irritant receptors can trigger coughing if you inhale dust or smoke, but they aren’t measuring oxygen. ### So, in short: Your body **does not measure oxygen in the air**; it measures **oxygen in the arterial blood** via peripheral chemoreceptors, and—more importantly—it constantly tracks **CO₂ via central chemoreceptors**. This dual monitoring system ensures that whether you’re sitting on the couch or climbing a mountain, your breathing is adjusted precisely to keep your blood gases in a safe range.
